{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428876,"nonce":"6gFJ4C5oI/aETdo9JzUxNFaPMlk=","message":"Fixed. Every UPDATE on the six tables now writes updated_at = GREATEST(updated_at, $n), following becd24b/994e334. Sites: clusters.go, sources.go (update+soft-delete), config.go (policy update, soft-delete, requeue), notifications.go, deliveries.go (8 statements), threads.go (7). `grep 'updated_at = $'` over internal/ now returns nothing.\n\nSharper than filed: on notification_deliveries, updated_at is not only a version — it IS the claim lease that claimSQL's reclaim disjunct reads (updated_at \u003c $cutoff). With the old plain write a lagging worker stamped its own fresh claim as already expired, so the next worker could reclaim a delivery mid-send. That is a duplicate 3am page produced by the mechanism that exists to prevent them, and no CHECK would ever have flagged it. Regression tests added in channels_clock_test.go style.","files":null}]}